if the colors of the leaves weren't  telling us otherwise you'd be forgiven  for thinking it was spring the warm  sunshine is confusing the plants and the  head Gardener here at birmingham's  Botanical Gardens  forecast to be 20 degrees today which is  about nine degrees warmer than it should  be this plant here is uh Paris commonly  called Forest Flame or flame of the  forest they normally grow their leaves  in the springtime the older leaves have  been sort of almost burnt off by the  Heat and the drought of the summertime  but then here we've got these young  bright green leaves which have been  growing during the Autumn so the plant  thinks it's spring it does indeed  and look around and you see a tourist in  a t-shirt summer flowers in full bloom  and a cactus collection that's normally  safely indoors from the start of October  still thriving in the mild Autumn  weather obviously worries all of us  doesn't it really just think well how  will it be in decades to come and it's  not just in the UK but across much of  Europe in Spain and in France people  have been basking in the Autumn sunshine  in Germany too it's still warm enough  for this so why is it so mild at the  moment we have the jet stream that's  running very actively across the UK and  that's leading to a series of low  pressure centers we're getting our warm  air from western parts of Europe around  Portugal Spain where it is also  exceptionally warm for the time of year  and all that warm air is coming across  the UK leading to temperatures being so  far above average at the moment  but many will see this unseasonal Autumn  and the scorching summer that went  before it as tangible evidence that our  climate is changing and with world  leaders due to meet next month for the  cop 27 climate Summit the Prime Minister  Rishi sunak has been criticized for  choosing not to attend due to what  Downing Street have described as other  pressing commitments  but the pressing issue here is where the  rare habitats will be affected the  National Trust is warning its chalk  grasslands are under threat temperatures  are forecast to drop closer to average  next month but fragile ecosystems need  predictable Seasons to survive Becky  Johnson Sky News
 

